  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical validation relies on a single French metropolitan area, meaning the specific spatial patterns of travel sensitivity may reflect Lyon's unique socioeconomic geography.",
      "what": "The study introduces a theoretical framework to estimate perceived accessibility by modifying a standard gravity-based indicator with individual travel cost sensitivity and an enhanced impedance parameter. This method was tested using disaggregated spatial data from a Household Transport Survey of over 11,000 households in Lyon, France, calibrated via a four-stage model. The resulting perceived accessibility scores were consistently lower than standard gravity-based accessibility measurements, showing distinct spatial variations such as lower travel sensitivity in the wealthy central business district compared to suburban zones.",
      "why": "It bridges the gap between objective spatial metrics and subjective travel experiences, demonstrating that standard accessibility models systematically overestimate how accessible destinations actually feel to different socioeconomic groups across a metropolitan area."
    }
  },
